1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Two buyers can pay the exact
same price for two different

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:07,160
properties and end up with
completely different levels of cost,

3
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:11,720
control and risk, depending on whether
they bought freehold or leasehold.

4
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:14,360
In this video, I'm going
to break down exactly what

5
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,480
changes between the two, so you
know exactly what you're buying.

6
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:20,210
I'm Jake, founder of Calibre Acquire.

7
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:23,420
I've worked in property for 14
years and transacted on over

8
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:26,920
£80 million of commercial property
investments in the past year alone.

9
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:30,970
I help investors get out of
underperforming assets into assets

10
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:32,720
that are actually going
to work harder for them.

11
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:36,840
Freehold means you own the building
and the land that it sits on outright.

12
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:40,640
There's no landlord above you, no
lease to run down and no ground rent.

13
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,210
You're free to make decisions about the
property without needing permission from

14
00:00:44,210 --> 00:00:48,840
anyone else, subject to planning and
any legal restrictions on the title.

15
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,550
Leasehold means you own the
right to use the property

16
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:54,560
for a fixed number of years
set out in the lease.

17
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:57,840
The freehold, the actual
land and building,

18
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:00,120
is owned by someone else, the freeholder.

19
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:04,480
You'll typically pay ground rent and,
depending on the lease, a service charge.

20
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,440
You may also need the freeholder's

21
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,880
permission for certain
changes to the property.

22
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,830
The practical differences
come down to a few things.

23
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:12,630
First, lease length.

24
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,730
A lease with under 80 years remaining

25
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:17,880
can affect mortgage
availability and resale value.

26
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,200
Under 70 years, this gets worse.

27
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,560
Then there's service charge.

28
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,040
This is the one investors
tend to underestimate.

29
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,190
It typically increases
every year, and unexpected

30
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:29,520
building costs get passed
on to leaseholders.

31
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,720
I've owned a flat where
a leak in the lift shaft

32
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:34,520
meant every resident had to
cover a share of the repair.

33
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:37,320
It ate into the return far
more than I'd planned for.

34
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:38,890
There's also ground rent.

35
00:01:38,890 --> 00:01:41,010
A smaller cost than service charge in most

36
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:43,960
cases, but still one that
doesn't exist for freehold.

37
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:45,120
And then there's control.

38
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:48,710
Alterations, subletting and
change of use can require

39
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:51,240
the freeholder's consent
on a leasehold property.

40
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:53,840
On a freehold, that
decision sits with you.

41
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,640
But freehold comes with
its own responsibilities.

42
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:00,440
As the freeholder, you're
responsible for all external works,

43
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:03,280
the roof, the structure,
the fabric of the building.

44
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:05,720
There's no freeholder above
to share those costs with.

45
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:09,560
A significant repair bill lands
entirely with you, and that needs

46
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,400
to be factored into how you
hold and manage the asset.

47
